      "id": 1,
      "text": "Caballo was founded in 1908 when John Gordon and his extended family homesteaded the Rio Grande Valley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caballo%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from the Caballo Mountains to the east, with 'caballo' meaning horse in Spanish.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caballo%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Caballo Dam began construction in June 1936 and finished in May 1938, creating a 343,990‑acre‑foot reservoir.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/caballo_new_mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "When the reservoir filled, residents had to move the town westward to higher ground.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caballo%2C_New_Mexico"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Caballo Reservoir stretches 15 miles along the Rio Grande, offering 50 miles of shoreline for boating, fishing, and kayaking.",
